get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c12eb3-55f1-5542-9408-dd989e93c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288494} {:answer/id #uuid "496b50b2-2381-5c0a-a5ec-57b315ce03f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288541} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289136} {:answer/id #uuid "e671bd82-67fb-5076-a0c6-d9a760f7d607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289562} {:answer/id #uuid "fae129e1-58dc-5648-9f67-66fc8378cba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed7f1-0992-5dbd-9e5b-68b34683d114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289136} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77a77dc-758e-557f-a490-659cf8cc382d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48699d28-6189-5c8c-ac56-4e78a856e54a" :question/math-academy-id "q-21500" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{5/2}+5{x}^{3/2}+15,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}+5\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{3/2}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{1/2}(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}=-\\frac{45}{4}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{-1/2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=-\\frac{45}{4}(1)^{1/2}+\\frac{15}{4}(1)^{-1/2}=-\\frac{15}{2}<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a643fc-b865-5596-8f40-9701cd340073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288174} {:answer/id #uuid "710f998a-91e7-56d8-bc7d-70a603ad78c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288819} {:answer/id #uuid "cc280798-2e6b-5ad9-add1-5725b15e8da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289389} {:answer/id #uuid "d3960910-1995-56c5-b003-a5bddf4a4554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289433} {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feca9e5b-047c-5d2d-861d-f5037ef6a710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289717} :answer-field/id #uuid "e49dfb32-835f-597e-bf8e-067b148c9379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b9fc172-9b76-5d55-bed8-430ab7579f16" :question/math-academy-id "q-21504" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=3$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+36x-48,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-12x+36,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+36 & =0 \\\\ -12(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=3.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(3)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=3,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=3$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=3\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1069597a-f1c7-525d-a156-29bb25411603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288155} {:answer/id #uuid "3357b431-6e9f-5a9b-aae2-0488eaab08f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288368} {:answer/id #uuid "973c8ff0-0dc4-5733-842f-ebc09a56e438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289043} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8f84b9-c506-55a7-9fe7-99e230ffc506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289189} {:answer/id #uuid "b871d291-b954-54c6-90f6-62a3670d5303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3357b431-6e9f-5a9b-aae2-0488eaab08f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288368} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8cc1a00-4c23-5c0e-957a-9be6d86b0490" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cf080c3-6e5f-55a7-a304-f18da95a7022" :question/math-academy-id "q-21508" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+9,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f13e36c-0129-5a1e-bd6e-c8aa07c9881d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1